# Wire number

The one door between JSON decimals and the factory's integers. Scores come in
as short decimals and the factory computes in basis points and micro-dollars, so
a number is read as an exact count of `10^-digits` units. Scores are read
EXACTLY — a finer value is refused, because a rank from a silently rounded input
cannot be reproduced. Money coming IN is rounded UP, so a budget is never
overspent by a fraction of a micro-dollar at a time.

## Note
Ten to the power `k`, for `k` at least zero.

## To tenTo (k: Int) -> Int:
    Let mutable out be 1.
    Repeat for i from 1 to k:
        Set out to out * 10.
    Return out.

## Note
A JSON number as an exact count of `10^-digits` units: `5.4` at three digits is
`5400`; `0.125` at two digits is refused.

## To jsonScaled (v: Json, digits: Int) -> Result of Int and Text:
    Inspect v:
        When JNum (n):
            Return a new Ok with value (n * tenTo(digits)).
        When JDec (m, sc):
            If 0 - sc is greater than digits:
                Return a new Err with error "{jsonSerialize(v)} has more than {digits} decimals".
            Return a new Ok with value (m * tenTo(digits + sc)).
        Otherwise:
            Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON number, found {jsonSerialize(v)}".
    Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON number".

## Note
A non-negative JSON number as a count of `10^-digits` units, rounded up.

## To jsonScaledCeil (v: Json, digits: Int) -> Result of Int and Text:
    Inspect v:
        When JDec (m, sc):
            If m is less than 0:
                Return a new Err with error "{jsonSerialize(v)} is negative".
            If 0 - sc is at most digits:
                Return jsonScaled(v, digits).
            Let unit be tenTo(0 - sc - digits).
            If m % unit is greater than 0:
                Return a new Ok with value (m / unit + 1).
            Return a new Ok with value (m / unit).
        Otherwise:
            If startsWith(jsonSerialize(v), "-"):
                Return a new Err with error "{jsonSerialize(v)} is negative".
            Return jsonScaled(v, digits).
    Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON number".

## Note
A decimal written as text (`0.0000025`, `2.5e-06`), the way OpenRouter lists
prices, read by the JSON number grammar and rounded up.

## To decimalTextScaledCeil (t: Text, digits: Int) -> Result of Int and Text:
    Inspect jsonTryParse(trim(t)):
        When JOk (v):
            Inspect v:
                When JStr (s):
                    Return a new Err with error "\"{t}\" is not a number".
                Otherwise:
                    Return jsonScaledCeil(v, digits).
        When JErr (e):
            Return a new Err with error "\"{t}\" is not a number".
    Return a new Err with error "\"{t}\" is not a number".

## Note
An integer folded onto `0 … size - 1`, so a property can range over every integer.

## To foldTo (n: Int, size: Int) -> Int:
    Let m be n % size.
    If m is less than 0:
        Return m + size.
    Return m.

## Test: decimals become exact scaled integers, and a finer one is refused

Expect that jsonScaled(jsonParse("5.4"), 3) is equal to a new Ok with value 5400.
Expect that jsonScaled(jsonParse("0.07"), 4) is equal to a new Ok with value 700.
Expect that jsonScaled(jsonParse("-1.5"), 1) is equal to a new Ok with value -15.
Expect that jsonScaled(jsonParse("0.125"), 2) is equal to a new Err with error "0.125 has more than 2 decimals".

## Test: money coming in is rounded up and never negative

Expect that jsonScaledCeil(jsonParse("0.0000012"), 6) is equal to a new Ok with value 2.
Expect that jsonScaledCeil(jsonParse("0.00000100"), 6) is equal to a new Ok with value 1.
Expect that jsonScaledCeil(jsonParse("1e-7"), 6) is equal to a new Ok with value 1.
Expect that jsonScaledCeil(jsonParse("3"), 6) is equal to a new Ok with value 3000000.
Expect that jsonScaledCeil(jsonParse("-0.5"), 6) is equal to a new Err with error "-0.5 is negative".

## Test: a price written as text is read by the JSON number grammar

Expect that decimalTextScaledCeil("0.0000025", 12) is equal to a new Ok with value 2500000.
Expect that decimalTextScaledCeil("2.5e-06", 12) is equal to a new Ok with value 2500000.
Expect that decimalTextScaledCeil("free", 12) is equal to a new Err with error "\"free\" is not a number".

## Test: a whole number rounds the same either way, and folding stays in range

For all booleans b:
    Let mutable n be 0.
    If b:
        Set n to 7.
    Expect that jsonScaledCeil(a new JNum with n n, 6) is equal to jsonScaled(a new JNum with n n, 6).
    Expect that foldTo(0 - n, 5) is at least 0.
